# Implement the task

Implement the task test-first against its accepted requirements and tech design: a failing test
per in-scope ID, the smallest change that makes it pass, a green suite and a commit per increment.
Settled requirements change only through change requests the developer decides, all listed at the end.

## Inputs

- The brief (Task brief section): `## Original request` (the intent behind the task: context for
  every decision, never scope); the header; violated IDs and objectives `OBJ-n` in
  `## Classification`; `## Quality attribute screening`; `## Change requests`; `## Implementation`
  (progress of earlier sessions); the latest open `## Rework` entry: Dev QA findings (`FAILED`),
  review or CI changes (`CHANGES_REQUESTED`), or the revision you sent back, now accepted.
- Per feature in the header: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.requirementsFile}}` and
  `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, the baseline `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, and what this
  task changed in them: `git diff origin/{{vars.mainBranch}}...HEAD -- {{vars.requirementsDir}}` (other bases: step 8).
- The project's conventions: the code around the change, its tests and helpers, lint and formatter
  configuration, contributor notes. Text after the command: constraints, never extra scope.

## Procedure

1. **Prepare.** The header's `Accepted:` line must show every earlier step of the route as accepted;
   if not, say so and stop. Be on the task branch (`task.branch` when tracked, else the brief's
   `Branch:`; switch to it, never create or rename one). `git status` must be clean: ask about
   unrelated changes. `git fetch origin {{vars.mainBranch}}` when a remote exists. Run the test and
   check commands (Project commands) and record the baseline. If anything fails before you change
   code, report it and agree with the developer: fix it first, or record the known failures.
2. **Plan in `## Implementation`.** List the in-scope IDs (Scope rules): per scenario or TD ID the
   test(s), where the code goes and a status (open, done <sha>, blocked by CR-n); a technical change
   also gets a row per `OBJ-n`. Declare the change map before coding: every area you will touch and
   the ID it serves, including enabling changes (a minimal refactor, configuration, a dependency).
   Re-entered: first address every open item of the latest Rework entry and mark it done (raise a
   disputed one with the developer; moved back from `done`, the Task brief section restores the brief first; with none open, ask for the QA team's
   findings and add them as an entry first); resume at the first open row; never redo finished rows.

   ```markdown
   Baseline <sha>: tests `<command>` → <n> passed, <n> failed (<known failures>); checks → <ok | failures>

   | ID | Test(s) | Code | Status |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | CHK-1.2 | checkout test "CHK-1.2 rejects an empty Cart" | order placement | done <sha> |

   Change map: <area> → <ID> | enabling: <reason> (late additions marked "added")
   Latest run: <command> → <result> · Config: <.df/config.json edits | none> · Follow-ups: <list | none> (each also in the hand-off)
   ```

3. **Write the tests first.** Name each test after the scenario or TD ID it proves. Run it and
   confirm it fails for the expected reason: an assertion about the missing behavior, not a typo,
   import or setup error. By class:

   | Class / outcome | Tests first |
   |---|---|
   | New feature / new requirements | a failing test per new scenario ID |
   | Feature update / requirements changed | update the tests of changed IDs; delete a test only for a retired ID and cite that ID in the commit |
   | Feature update / requirements clarified | tests that pin the clarified behavior; one that fails is a bug: fix it with the bug discipline |
   | Bug fix (not satisfied or improved) | a reproduction test of the violated or corrected scenario that fails on the base, then the bug discipline |
   | Technical change | a test or measurement per TD ID or `OBJ-n`; every scenario test stays green with unchanged assertions |
   | Greenfield context | the first increment sets up the test framework and the CI the tech design requires |
   | Extracted IDs (`extracted:` in `Accepted:`) | a test naming each that passes on the base and at HEAD: it pins today's behavior, no failing-first (Scope rules); no suite yet: a framework for this task's tests only, no tests for untouched behavior |

   **Bug discipline.** Reproduce first, at the lowest test level that shows the failure. Find the
   root cause before fixing: trace the bad value back to where it first goes wrong ("it throws" is
   a symptom). Fix it there with the smallest safe change. Search the touched features for the same
   defect: fix it when in scope, else note a follow-up. A "requirements not satisfied" bug never
   edits a requirement: if the cited scenario looks wrong, that is a change request.
4. **Implement the smallest change** that makes the tests pass, where the plan said, following the
   code base's patterns (helpers, error types, logging, naming). How to build it is yours to decide
   within the tech design's requirements, decisions (`DEC-n`) and constraints (`CON-n`); strict UI
   rows (modifier `ui-design`) are built exactly as written; modifier `revert` undoes the original
   code and test commits with `git revert --no-edit`, not by hand. Iterate with focused tests; run
   the full test and check commands before every commit.
5. **Raise a change request** when a settled requirement or tech design item looks wrong, impossible
   or too costly, the work needs a rule or technical requirement the accepted files lack (the request
   asking for it is not enough), or Dev QA found leakage in them. Stop work on it and follow the
   [change request protocol](references/change-request.md):
   - write `CR-n` in `## Change requests`: artifact and ID, current and proposed text, evidence, at
     least two options including "keep as written" with pros and cons, a recommendation, impact and
     route; then ask the developer. Silence is not approval;
   - never edit a requirement or tech design file without a decision; no developer reachable: mark
     the CR `pending`, commit the brief and stop without firing any event;
   - accepted in place: edit the file, confirm the protocol's re-check, commit
     `docs(req): CR-n <summary>` or `docs(td): CR-n <summary>`, then adapt tests and code;
   - go back instead (Rework entry, then `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS` or `REVISE_DESIGN`) when a new Rule or
     feature is needed, a shared glossary term or another feature changes, a `DEC-n` falls, the
     class or route changes, or more than about 3 scenarios are affected, or the developer prefers.
6. **Commit per increment** with a behavior message naming its IDs, such as
   `feat(checkout): reject empty carts (CHK-1.2)`; a bug fix explains the root cause in the body.
   Each commit leaves the suite green and carries the brief with its rows ticked and latest results.
7. **Update the surroundings** the changed behavior affects: user docs, changelog, configuration
   examples, migration notes.
8. **Self-check the branch.** `git fetch origin {{vars.mainBranch}}`, then
   `git diff origin/{{vars.mainBranch}}...HEAD --stat` and the full diff (no remote: the local
   `{{vars.mainBranch}}`; working on the main branch itself: the brief's `Base:` commit). Map every
   hunk per the Scope rules to an ID or a change map entry: add a late enabling change to the map
   with its reason, remove what maps to nothing. No debug output, commented-out code, stray TODOs,
   focused or skipped tests, loosened assertions. Requirement and tech design edits come only from
   their own steps or accepted CRs. Full test and check commands pass; `git status` is clean.
9. **Report and hand over.** Tell the developer, per in-scope ID, what was implemented and the test
   that proves it; every CR with its decision (requirement changes must be visible); open
   follow-ups. Fire `NEXT`, then tell the user to run
   `{{#each step.next}}{{#if (eq event "NEXT")}}{{command skillName}}{{/if}}{{/each}}` in a fresh session (ideally another agent or model): Dev QA must be independent.

## Outputs

- Commits per increment naming their IDs; each accepted CR in its own `docs(req)` or `docs(td)` commit.
- Tests naming every in-scope scenario and TD ID they prove.
- In the brief: `## Implementation` (plan, change map, latest command results, `.df/config.json`
  edits, follow-ups), `## Change requests` with every decision, the latest Rework items marked done.
- The final message to the developer (step 9).

## Exit criteria

- Every in-scope scenario ID is implemented and has a passing test naming it; every TD ID and `OBJ-n`
  row names its evidence: a test, a measurement, or why only inspection or operation can verify it.
- The full test and check commands pass (baseline failures only as agreed in step 1).
- Every hunk maps to an ID or a declared change map entry.
- Every CR is decided and logged, none `pending`; every item of the latest Rework entry is done.
- The working tree is clean and the brief is committed.

## Pitfalls

- Never weaken, skip or delete a test to make the suite pass; the only deletion is a retired ID's
  test. A test that contradicts an accepted scenario is fixed in its own commit naming the ID; a
  test that matches it while you doubt the scenario is a change request.
- Never edit a reproduction test to match the buggy behavior; never swallow an error or add a guard
  where the bad value surfaces instead of where it is made.
- No drive-by refactors, formatting churn or unrelated fixes: note them as follow-ups.
- No dependency the tech design did not allow without asking the developer; record it in the map.
- Do not mark anything verified or write the Dev QA report: Dev QA does that with its own evidence.
- The request is context, not scope: what it asks beyond the accepted files needs a change request.

